# Volt sports redesign — 2026-10-31

VSR01 lowers the windshield base and roof substantially, making the hood shallower. VSR02 replaces the inflated door cross-section with a narrower waist, defined shoulders and a tucked lower flank. VSR03 finishes the fascia, lamps, bonnet, wheels and rear. VSR04 refreshes the native prepared assets and pictures. The owner's request for a substantial sporty electric-car redesign supersedes the earlier modest-cowl-height and completely closed lower-bumper presentation.

[Matched before/after views](evidence/volt-sport-redesign-2026-10-31/before-after.html) use the actual Garage and the same camera recipes. The [front three-quarter](evidence/volt-sport-redesign-2026-10-31/final-gl/front-quarter-low-clean.png), [side](evidence/volt-sport-redesign-2026-10-31/final-gl/side-opposite-clean.png), and [rear](evidence/volt-sport-redesign-2026-10-31/final-mobile/rear-low-clean.png) show the resulting proportions. Both desktop renderers have eighteen clean/worn final views each.

The actual detailed buffers measure:

| Measurement | Task start | New model | Change at world scale |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Windscreen foot, authored Y | 0.9700 | 0.8407 | 11.4 cm lower |
| Roof maximum, authored Y | 1.2930 | 1.1821 | 9.8 cm lower |
| Hood centre, nose-to-cowl angle | 13.047° | 6.929° | 46.9% shallower |

The hood nose rises only about 1 cm at world scale; lowering the cowl accounts for the flatter profile. The front fender retains clearance above the wheel aperture while its centre hood stays below the shoulder. The new waist rises gently and linearly toward the rear haunch. Handles and mirrors follow the lowered surfaces. The five runtime sources are `electric_car_model.gd`, `electric_reference_body.gd`, `electric_reference_cabin.gd`, `electric_reference_details.gd`, and `electric_reference_mesh.gd`.

The fascia now has a formed central lower opening and two corner recesses with inset backs and connected returns. Six small projector optics sit within shaped smoked lamp housings beneath the light guide. The bonnet has a fitted perimeter shut line. Swept wheel faces have machined edges and a centre channel; the open rim ring exposes the brake disc and bronze caliper. A rear valance, five diffuser fences and a small painted deck lip finish the tail. Materials retain the existing saved-paint, pattern and weathering systems.

The new actual-buffer regression passes 98/98 procedurally and 104/104 with prepared meshes, including exact same-process parity across all 289 prepared meshes. It checks the larger cowl/roof reductions, shallow monotonic hood, finite unit normals, wheel-aperture clearance, body/glass joins, physically recessed bumper returns, upper-nose coverage across the separate painted pieces, saved paint/patterns, wheel pivots and night-light attachment. The task-start control fails 22 checks, including all three proportional targets in each model. The original sport nose 8/8, corrected optics 16/16 procedural and prepared, Electric Garage-template 4/4, preview identity 5/5 and racing integration 23/23 pass. The Garage-template test is a preserved copy of the existing fixture restricted to Electric with the actual editable-kit setting. Actual App Garage/race checks pass 58/58 on Compatibility and 58/58 on desktop Mobile, with nine captures each. Their logs retain frame hitches, so these are functional checks rather than performance results. The [Garage](evidence/volt-sport-redesign-2026-10-31/app-gl/garage-1280.png) and [production race view](evidence/volt-sport-redesign-2026-10-31/app-mobile/race.png) show the model in the application.

The unchanged historical redesign fixture passes 138/150. Its twelve failures require the single old `ReferenceFrontUpper` mesh to cover Y=0.34/0.48: the first is now the intended cooling opening, and the second belongs to the separate centre bumper piece. The new regression verifies the opening's physical depth/returns and the union of painted pieces at Y=0.48/0.50/0.62. No old assertion was removed or relaxed. The first intermediate model exceeded the 120,000-triangle budget; reducing rear-facing spoke tessellation and broad panel subdivisions restored the budget. A real 6.8 mm lamp-housing/wrap mismatch was found and fixed by giving the housing its own centre height. An initial new-test node-name lookup used periods that Godot converts to underscores; its corrected lookup retains the same geometry checks. Initial results remain in the evidence directory.

Final visible geometry is 118,584 detailed / 36,600 simple triangles, with 3,054 separate simple shadow triangles. The dedicated `electric_sport_fastback_v1` resources, five shadow proxies, Electric Garage template and 52 Electric pictures were rebuilt. The existing mesh-root picture identity rejects older persistent Volt pictures. Only the Electric Garage template was regenerated; other cars' templates may rebuild under the shared editor source guard. The pinned editor imported all 52 pictures successfully.

Commands, exit codes, source/asset hashes and the task-only source checkpoint are kept in [the evidence report](evidence/volt-sport-redesign-2026-10-31/report.json). Every engine command used the crash-dialog wrapper and an isolated offline profile. Windows root-certificate-store warnings remain in the logs. Desktop rendering and automated checks do not establish phone performance or owner appearance acceptance. AAA is the owner's visual-quality target, not a certification from these measurements. Reload/rebuild and owner review remain. No device query/test, APK, owner-save/live/backend mutation, deployment, owner-process restart or main-index staging/commit/merge was performed. Concurrent work and prior Electric mesh directories were preserved.
